div.p:
1. Authors do not agree in enumerating the meanings of faith. —
Although, as I have said, the word faith is multivalent and ambiguous,
nonetheless authors — even Catholics — do not agree in enumerating its meanings;
for Alexander of Hales, in Part 3, q. 64, memb. 1, enumerates eleven meanings of faith.
Bonaventure, however, in Book 3, dist. 23, dub. 2, on the text, posits only ten,
since he reduces the fifth and the last into one;
and Gabriel holds the same view there at q. 2,
for although he first simply reports and appears to follow Hales,
he afterwards imitates Bonaventure in his enumeration;
but Vega, in question 1 on Justification at the beginning, posits nine meanings,
adding some not found in the preceding authors and omitting some of theirs;
and in much the same way Castro, Against Heresies, s.v. Faith,
reduces these meanings to seven.

Likewise Roffensis and Albert Pighius, in the passage cited;
but Soto, book 2 on Nature and Grace, ch. 6, attacks this multiplication
and admits only two meanings of faith —
namely, insofar as it signifies either fidelity (fidelitatem) or assent founded on some testimony;
and Medina holds much the same, in book 1, On Right Faith toward God, ch. 1.
But although perhaps these two meanings are the principal ones,
we cannot nonetheless deny that this word has been extended to signify many other things,
which it is necessary to declare briefly,
because it will be very useful and opportune for refuting errors.

3. That faith referred to the will implies fidelity (fidelitatem) and trust (fiduciam). — On fidelity (fidelitate). —
Second, it must be said that the word faith, even insofar as it signifies a perfection of the will, signifies several things;
this can be proved, because this word signifies both fidelity (fidelitatem) and trust (fiduciam).
But neither of these is equally certain, and therefore each must be treated separately.
First, then, it is most well-known that fidelity (fidelitatem) pertains to the will;
for it is a certain moral virtue that provides constancy in keeping promises;
this virtue, therefore, is called faith by the Latins;
hence Cicero, in the first book of the Offices:
Faith is the constancy and truth of what has been said and agreed upon;
indeed, he conjectures there that faith was so called because through it what is said is done,
suggesting also that this was the original imposition of the word —
a view also endorsed by Augustine in the passages cited, and in his third Letter to Jerome,
and followed by Isidore;
and among jurists, and especially in civil law, this meaning is frequent.
In divine Scripture as well this meaning is found throughout:
Leviticus 6: The soul that has denied a deposit committed to its faith shall make restitution, etc. —
that is, to its fidelity (fidelitati);
Ecclesiasticus 27: He who reveals secrets loses faith — that is, fidelity (fidelitatem);
and the Councils and Fathers commonly understand it in this way,
as I said when treating of vows, book one, ch. 13, and book 4, chapter one,
in keeping with 1 Timothy 5[:12]: Having condemnation,
because they made void their first faith — that is, the fidelity (fidelitatem) owed by vow.
Finally, faith is attributed to God in this way:
Romans 3, Has their unbelief made the faith of God without effect? —
and from this God is called faithful,
for God is not capable of faith in the proper intellectual sense.

1. Although We Must Treat of Faith of the Intellect,
the Pious Affect of the Will Is Not Excluded. —
I presuppose here that the faith of the will is not treated,
even if it be infused;
for if the discourse concerns trust (fiducia),
admitting the name of faith in that signification,
its consideration pertains to the doctrine on hope,
because, as I said, it is not distinguished from it.
But if we speak of fidelity (fidelitate),
that saying of Augustine, in the book On the Spirit and the Letter, ch. 31,
aptly applies:
We are now speaking of that faith which we employ when we believe something,
not that which we give when we promise something;
for this faith is a moral virtue and a potential part of justice,
and therefore pertains to its treatment.
But when we say here that the faith of the will is not treated,
we speak of that faith which, in its proper notion, is consummated in the will;
nonetheless we do not exclude that will
which the Council of Orange called the beginning of faith;
for that is of present consideration,
insofar as the faith of the intellect itself is voluntary;
yet that will is not faith, but a pious affect toward faith.

5. The Order and Distribution of This Treatise. —
From all this it is finally clear what order we must follow
in treating this matter;
for since we treat of the virtue of faith,
we must also necessarily speak of the vices contrary to it,
and therefore we divide the matter into two principal parts:
the first will be on the virtue, which is more speculative;
the second on the vices, which is almost entirely moral,
since in it must be considered the precepts of faith,
and the sins by which they are violated,
and the penalties that correspond to them.
The former part we will divide into four members:
the first will be on the object, because from it the species of the virtue is taken;
the second on the act, because through it the habit is also specified;
the third on the habit; and the fourth on the things annexed to the virtue of faith.
